title: Lenin Falls Silent
subtitle: A third stroke takes Lenin's speech, and Moscow divides.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1923 and month = 3 and comintern_member = 1
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
RG.news('Lenin suffers a third stroke and can no longer speak', 3);
RG.ecci('lenin', -10);
!}

= Lenin Falls Silent

On 10 March Lenin suffers a third stroke. He lives, at Gorki, but he cannot speak, and he will not write again. His last articles, dictated in the winter, were against bureaucracy, against the Workers' and Peasants' Inspection, and, those close to him say, against the general secretary of the party, Joseph Stalin, who has been answering the letters.

The men who are left do not trust each other. Zinoviev, President of the International, and Kamenev have joined Stalin in a triumvirate against Trotsky, who has the Red Army and the reputation. Radek, who knows Germany best, is nobody's man. Bukharin edits *Pravda* and is courted by everyone.

The German party lives on the Executive's letters and the Executive's money. Whoever wins in Moscow will decide what those letters say.

- @zinoviev: Report to Zinoviev, as the President of the International.
- @trotsky: Send the Zentrale's reports to Trotsky.
- @radek: Keep to Radek, who knows Germany.
- @aloof: Keep the German party out of the Russian quarrel.

@zinoviev
subtitle: The President is pleased, and he likes the Left. Trotsky notices.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 10);
RG.ecci('trotsky', -4);
Q.independence -= 3;
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 3;
Q.lean_offensive += 1;
!}

The Zentrale's monthly report goes to the President's office first. Zinoviev answers it himself, at length, with compliments for the Berlin district.

@trotsky
subtitle: Trotsky wants a German revolution and a date for it. The triumvirs keep a list.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('trotsky', 10);
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -6);
Q.red_guards_arms += 2;
Q.lean_unionist += 1;
!}

Trotsky reads German reports with a pencil and returns them with questions about rifles, railway junctions and the loyalty of the Reichswehr's sergeants. In the Kremlin, someone notes who is writing to him.

@radek
subtitle: The Right's friend in Moscow. Radek defends us; nobody defends Radek.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('radek', 8);
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -2);
Q.unionist_satisfaction += 3;
Q.lean_unionist += 1;
!}

Radek promises to keep the German question out of the Russian one. It is a promise he cannot keep, and he knows it.

@aloof
subtitle: The Spartacist answer. More independence, and nobody in Moscow owes us anything.
on-arrival: {!
Q.independence += 5;
RG.ecci('all', -3);
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 4;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
!}

The Zentrale resolves that the German party has no view on the internal questions of the Russian party. In Moscow, every faction reads that as a view.
